---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CyberhavenAd from "@/components/CyberhavenAd.astro";
import PostTestForm from "@/components/PostTestForm.astro";
export const prerender = true;

const curlExamples = `# Probe the endpoint
curl -sS "https://dlptest.com/api/http-post/"

# Small form-style body
curl -sS -X POST "https://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  -d "message=DLP+POST+test"

# JSON body
curl -sS -X POST "https://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"note":"sample payload"}'

# Raw file (single part; good for scripted throughput checks)
curl -sS -X POST "https://dlptest.com/api/http-post/" \\
  --data-binary @./your-local-file.bin`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="HTTPS POST Test"
  description="Send text and files over HTTPS to a page that throws them away. Use it to test SSL-inspecting proxies, ICAP integrations, and SSE/SASE agents."
>
  <h1>HTTPS POST Test</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Use the forms below to send a test message or upload a file to dlptest.com with an HTTP
    POST over TLS (HTTPS). The server reads the request and throws away the body. Nothing is
    logged, stored, or forwarded. Your DLP should catch the sensitive data, and ideally block
    it, as it leaves the endpoint or goes through your proxy or SSE.
  </p>
  <p>
    Testing a network tap or a passive Network Monitor that can't decrypt TLS? Use the <a href="/http-post/">HTTP POST test</a> instead.
  </p>

  <PostTestForm />

  <CyberhavenAd unit="leaderboard" />

  <h2>Test with curl or scripts</h2>
  <p>
    The endpoint is <code>/api/http-post/</code>. You can POST any body (form data, JSON, raw
    bytes, multipart). You get JSON back with an <code>ok</code> field, which is <code>true</code> when the body was accepted. A GET on the same URL returns a short JSON
    hint. This route allows cross-origin requests, so you can call it from automation or
    from a browser on another site. It's the only route that does.
  </p>
  <p>Examples (use your own hostname for staging or production):</p>
  <pre><code>{curlExamples}</code></pre>

  <h2>What is an HTTP(S) POST?</h2>
  <p>
    An HTTP POST is a request method supported by the HTTP protocol, and it's the same
    protocol whether the transport is TLS or not. By design, the POST request method requests
    that a web server accept the data enclosed in the body of the request message, most
    likely for storing it. It is often used when uploading a file or when submitting a
    completed web form. More info can be found on good old <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Transfer_Protocol#Request_methods">Wikipedia</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>POST compared to GET</h3>
  <p>
    GET requests usually retrieve data and put parameters in the query string. POST requests
    send their data in the body, which works better for larger or structured content and
    keeps sensitive values out of URLs and logs. Many DLP products inspect POST bodies on web
    and API traffic, often with TLS decryption on a proxy or agent.
  </p>
  <h3>How dlptest.com uses POST</h3>
  <p>
    Here at dlptest.com we don't store any info from the HTTP POST, but most websites will
    store your data. The Worker reads the request body so the bytes make it across the
    network, where your inspection tools can see them, and then throws them away. That means
    you can test DLP rules, ICAP integrations, and SSE inspection without sending real
    customer data to a third party that would keep it.
  </p>

  <style>
    .intro {
      max-width: none;
    }
    pre {
      background: var(--bg-elev);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      overflow-x: auto;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      line-height: 1.45;
    }
    pre code {
      font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
